The Problem: Enrollment Eligibility Checker
The IT313 registrar has given you a raw list of enrollee records for the semester. Your task is to write a small
Node.js program that turns this raw data into a formatted eligibility report, in the style of a real registrar utility.
Starter data (place this in your main script or a separate data file):
const enrollees = [
 { name: "Ana Cruz", prelim: 85, midterm: 90, final: 88 },
 { name: "Bea Santos", prelim: 70, midterm: 65, final: 60 },
 { name: "Cid Ramos", prelim: 95, midterm: 92, final: 97 },
 { name: "Dex Alonzo", prelim: 60, midterm: 55, final: 50 },
 { name: "Eli Tan", prelim: 78, midterm: 80, final: 76 },
];
Passing standard: an enrollee is PASSING if their average of prelim, midterm, and final is 75 or above; otherwise
they are on PROBATION.
Sample expected output (your formatting may vary slightly, but the information must match):
=== IT313 Enrollment Eligibility Report ===
Ana Cruz - Average: 87.67 - PASSING
Bea Santos - Average: 65.00 - PROBATION
Cid Ramos - Average: 94.67 - PASSING
Dex Alonzo - Average: 55.00 - PROBATION
Eli Tan - Average: 78.00 - PASSING
Class Average: 76.07
Passing: 3 / 5
Requirements — your solution must:
1. Create a separate module file (e.g., gradeUtils.js) that exports a named function computeAverage(prelim,
midterm, final) and a default function isPassing(average), then import both into your main script using ES
module import/export syntax.
2. Write a function getEnrollees() that returns a Promise which resolves with the enrollees array after a short delay
(e.g., using setTimeout), simulating a call to a registrar API.
3. Write an async function that awaits getEnrollees() inside a try/catch block, so a failed "connection" is caught
and reported instead of crashing the program.
4. Use destructuring to pull name, prelim, midterm, and final out of each enrollee record where you use them.
5. Use .map(), together with your imported computeAverage() and isPassing(), to build a new array of { name,
average, status } objects.
6. Use .filter() to separate the passing enrollees from those on probation.
7. Use .reduce() to compute the class average across all enrollees.
8. Use template literals to print the formatted report shown above, including each enrollee's line, the class average,
and the passing count.
9. Include a README.md in your project explaining the problem, your approach, and how to run gradeUtils.js
and your main script.